Wait — `void tail?.then(...)` is an unhandled pattern; also complexity. Simplify: track chain as `{current}` and at the end, set a cleanup that deletes the map entry if it's still the same promise. Actually simpler: keep a counter incrementing; store the tail promise; after completion, if the map entry === the tail, delete it. The unhandled rejection: previous could reject? No — `previous` chain never rejects because we always `await previous` and our `ours` resolves always (release() in finally, never rejects). So tail never rejects. But `saveChains.get(path) === tail` where tail is `previous.then(() => ours)` — the value stored in map is that tail. OK.

But there's a subtle bug: the stored value is `tail` itself; after our run finishes, another save may have already queued (map holds newer tail). Then we don't delete. Good. The `void tail?.then(...)` — tail is guaranteed defined. Let me simplify with cleaner code.

Also in-process ordering issue: two saves queued — second one merges with disk anyway, so even if both run sequentially that's fine.

Now the in-process chain: `saveState` is called with our in-memory snapshot `state` each time; since each call re-reads disk and merges, lost updates across processes are handled.

Concern: merge semantics for history — my merge keeps theirs entries not in ours, then ours entries. Suppose process A recorded 'x' then 'y'; process B recorded 'x' then 'y' too. Merge fine. Suppose A: [a, b], B: [b, a] (both same set) — ours appended order; ok. Suppose A: [1,2], B: [1,3] → merged: theirs(B) entries not in ours(A): [3], plus ours [1,2] → [3,1,2]. Hmm — ordering altered but no data lost; acceptable trade.

However one issue: in `rememberPrompt`, the controller persists `{ ...this.persisted.history, [key]: history.entries }` where history.entries is the *current browse state entries* — fine.

But there's a subtlety: after merge, controller's `this.persisted` is stale relative to disk (another process may have added entries). Controller should adopt the merged snapshot returned by saveState. Let me update persist():

```ts
private persist(patch): void {
    this.persisted = { ...this.persisted, ...patch };
    const snapshot = this.persisted;
    void this.track('save', this.deps.saveLocalState(snapshot).then((result) => {
      // Adopt what was actually written so later patches build on it.
      if (this.persisted === snapshot) this.persisted = result.state;
    }));
}
```

But saveLocalState return type currently `Promise<void>` in deps interface. I'll change the dep signature to `Promise<SaveResult>` — but many tests pass `saveLocalState: async () => {}` returning undefined. Changing signature breaks tests (type error). Options: make controller not depend on result, or update tests. Simpler: keep dep signature accepting `Promise<SaveResult | void>`? That's ugly.

Alternative: controller keeps its own persisted but merges could still lose? Scenario: Process A and B both write. A persists snapshot A1 (merged result M1 written to disk, but A's persisted is still A1). B concurrently persists B1; B reads disk — but A's write may not have landed yet when B reads. Suppose A's save runs: reads disk D0, merges → M1, writes. B's save runs concurrently but before A's rename: reads D0, merges → M2 (missing A's history entry from M1 if A added a new entry not in D0), writes M2 after A → A's new entry lost!

Wait — but in-process chain only serializes within a process. Cross-process: both read D0 → both write → last wins, losing the other's *new* entries (entries created in A1 not present in D0 or B1). Hmm. So the read-merge-write reduces but doesn't fully eliminate lost updates unless the merge happens under the lock and both read after each other... No: A acquires lock, reads D0, merges A1, writes, releases. B acquires lock *after A releases* (or before). If B acquires after A, B reads D0? No — B reads after acquiring lock, and A has written by then (A writes before releasing). So B reads M1, merges with B1 → M3 containing both. 

The lock orders the whole read-merge-write critical section. So cross-process atomicity holds: either A's section runs entirely before B's, or vice versa. Each section: read current disk → merge with own snapshot → write. In that order, no lost updates (as long as merge is correct). My earlier worry was unfounded because read happens inside the lock. 

Remaining subtlety: controller's `this.persisted` staleness: if B adopts merged M3 internally but controller keeps A1... controller's own next persist patches A1 — e.g. persist({history: {...A1.history, [key]: entries}}) — other keys from disk (written by B) would be dropped from the patch! E.g. A's persisted A1 = {history: {A: [...]}}. Disk after B: {history: {A: [...], B: [...]}}. A next records a prompt in project A: patch history {A: newentries} → persisted becomes {history: {A: new}} — B's project B history GONE from the snapshot passed to saveState. But saveState merges with disk: disk has B; ours lacks B; merged keeps theirs B entries. Phew — the merge saves us: `theirs` (disk) contributes keys not in ours. Yes! mergeState keeps theirs-only keys. So no data loss even if controller's persisted is stale. 

However, controller's in-memory `this.state.history` for project A reflects only A's entries — fine, that's per-project.

But one flaw: mergeState for a key present in ours but with fewer entries than theirs — e.g. ours A: [x], theirs A: [x, y] where y was recorded by B in the same project dir. Merged: theirs not in ours: [y], plus ours: [x] → [y, x]. Order slightly off but complete. Ideally controller adopts merged state to keep ordering; but adopting merged state wholesale into controller requires threading result through track(). I can do it: persist() can await the save and update this.persisted = result.state if unchanged. Let me implement adopting carefully:

```ts
private persist(patch): void {
  this.persisted = { ...this.persisted, ...patch };
  const snapshot = this.persisted;
  void this.track('save', this.deps.saveLocalState(snapshot).then((result) => {
    if (result?.state !== undefined && this.persisted === snapshot) {
      this.persisted = result.state;
      // also refresh in-memory history view if not browsing? Simpler: leave UI as-is;
      // persisted metadata only.
    }
